# 3. Global Timestamp Standard

All observations must eventually contain a standardized timestamp.

Primary timestamp field:

`timestamp`

Target timezone:

`America/New_York`

Example:

```text
2026-10-01 10:35:00-04:00
```

Raw source timestamps must be preserved where practical.

Recommended raw fields:

| Field | Description |
|---|---|
| `timestamp_raw` | Original timestamp supplied by source |
| `timestamp` | Standardized timestamp |
| `timezone_source` | Original timezone |
| `timezone_standard` | America/New_York |

Daylight Saving Time must be handled using timezone-aware datetime objects rather than manually subtracting fixed UTC offsets.

---

# 4. Observation Frequency

The primary modeling interval will be:

\[
5\text{ minutes}
\]

Example:

```text
09:30
09:35
09:40
09:45
...
15:55
16:00
```

Higher-frequency source data may be aggregated to 5-minute intervals.

Lower-frequency data must not be artificially treated as new information every five minutes.

---

# 5. Trading Session

The initial equity-market modeling session will generally be:

```text
09:30 ET – 16:00 ET
```

Regular market holidays and early-close sessions must be identified.

Extended-hours observations may be stored but should initially remain separate from the primary modeling dataset.

Treasury futures and other markets may trade outside these hours, but only information available at the prediction timestamp may be used.

